namespace IPRO.DataAccess;

// 499 (2026-09-18): the Insurance / Financial and Mortgage editions' starter libraries -- eight
// articles each, in two categories each. Until now those two editions shipped with two articles
// apiece while Accountants had twenty-odd and Generic eight, and the public pages say the articles
// "are written for your market".
//
// These are REGULATED subjects, so the rules are stricter than the Generic library's and are pinned
// by EditionLibraries499Tests: general information only, never advice; no figures, rates, limits,
// years or prices (they go stale and they differ by province); no promise of any outcome; a
// pre-approval is never described as a commitment to lend; tax and legal questions are pointed to a
// tax professional or a lawyer; Canadian terms throughout; and only plain markup (p, h3, ul, li,
// strong). Every adviser can rewrite any of them on their own site, and SuperAdmin edits the
// starters under Starter Content. WebsiteStarterArticleSeeder adds them per title, so they reach a
// database that was seeded long ago.
public static class EditionStarterArticles
{
    public static readonly GenericStarterArticles.Entry[] InsuranceFinancial =
    {
        new("Term or Permanent Life Insurance: How to Think About the Choice",
            "Protection",
            "A plain-language look at what term and permanent life insurance are each for, and the questions that help separate them.",
            """
            <p>Almost all life insurance is either term or permanent. Both protect the people who depend on you, in different ways, and understanding the difference makes any later conversation easier.</p><h3>What term insurance is for</h3><p>Term insurance covers you for a set period that you choose at the start. If you die while the coverage is in force, the insurer pays the death benefit to the people you've named. If the term ends and you're still here, the coverage ends too, unless the contract lets you renew or convert it.</p><p>People usually think of term insurance for needs that have an end date: a mortgage, a business loan, the years until children can support themselves. Because it covers a limited period and builds no savings, the premium for a given amount of coverage usually starts out lower than it would for permanent insurance. Premiums typically rise if you renew at an older age.</p><h3>What permanent insurance is for</h3><p>Permanent insurance is designed to stay in force for your whole life, as long as the premiums are paid as the contract requires. There are several kinds, and they work differently. Some contracts include a savings or cash-value component that can grow over time and that you may be able to access while you're alive, subject to the contract's terms.</p><p>People usually think of permanent insurance for needs that don't end: final expenses, taxes or other costs an estate may face, a gift to family or to a charity, or support for a dependant who will always need care. The trade-off is cost and complexity. Premiums are generally higher for the same amount of coverage, and the contracts take more time to understand.</p><h3>The questions that separate them</h3><ul><li><strong>How long will the need last?</strong> A need with an end date and a need that lasts a lifetime point in different directions.</li><li><strong>What fits the budget?</strong> Coverage only helps if the premiums stay affordable year after year.</li><li><strong>What are your estate goals?</strong> Leaving something behind, or covering costs that arise at death, is a different job from replacing an income.</li><li><strong>How much does flexibility matter to you?</strong> Some term contracts can be converted to permanent coverage without new medical questions. Whether that's available, and until when, is set out in the contract.</li></ul><h3>It isn't always one or the other</h3><p>Many people hold both kinds at different stages of life. A family might carry term coverage while the mortgage and the children are the main concern, alongside a smaller permanent policy meant for the long run. As debts shrink and children grow up, the mix often changes.</p><p>Neither kind is better in general. Each suits certain needs, and the details vary from one contract to the next. Tax treatment can matter too, particularly where cash value or an estate is involved, and that's a question for us or a tax professional.</p><p>If you'd like to talk through how these ideas apply to your own situation, we're glad to have that conversation whenever you're ready.</p>
            """),
        new("What Disability Insurance Covers, and Why It Is Often Overlooked",
            "Protection",
            "Why income is the asset most people forget to protect, and the policy terms worth understanding in any disability coverage.",
            """
            <p>Most people insure their home and their car as a matter of course. Far fewer think about insuring the thing that pays for both: the ability to earn an income.</p><p>Over a working lifetime, your income is likely the largest financial asset you have. It pays the mortgage or rent, fills the fridge and funds every savings goal. Disability insurance exists to replace part of that income if an illness or injury keeps you from working.</p><h3>Why it gets overlooked</h3><p>Disability feels unlikely in a way that other risks don't. People picture a dramatic accident, when in practice many claims come from ordinary illnesses, back and joint problems, and mental health conditions. It's also easy to assume that workplace benefits or government programs will cover everything. Often they cover less than people expect, or for a shorter time.</p><h3>The ideas that shape every policy</h3><ul><li><strong>The definition of disability.</strong> This is the test the insurer uses to decide whether you qualify for benefits. Some contracts ask whether you can do the main duties of your own occupation. Others ask whether you can do any work you're reasonably suited for by education, training or experience. Some change from one test to the other after a period of time. The wording matters a great deal.</li><li><strong>The waiting period.</strong> This is the time between becoming disabled and the start of benefit payments. A longer waiting period generally means a lower premium, and it also means relying on savings or other resources for longer.</li><li><strong>The benefit period.</strong> This is how long payments can continue if you remain disabled. Some contracts pay for a limited number of years, and others can pay until a stated age.</li></ul><h3>Workplace coverage and personal coverage</h3><p>Group coverage through an employer is valuable, and for many people it's the only disability coverage they have. It also has limits. Group plans usually end when you leave the job. The employer or insurer can change the plan. The benefit may be based on salary alone, leaving out bonuses, commissions or self-employment income, and it may be reduced by payments from other sources.</p><p>A personal policy is one you own yourself. It goes with you from job to job, and its terms are set out in your own contract. It also involves medical and financial questions when you apply, and you pay the premium yourself. How benefits are taxed can vary with how the coverage is set up, so that's worth raising with us or a tax professional.</p><h3>Questions to ask about a policy</h3><ul><li>How does this contract define disability, and does the definition change over time?</li><li>How long is the waiting period, and how long can benefits be paid?</li><li>What happens if I can work part-time, or return to work gradually?</li><li>What is excluded or limited?</li><li>Can the insurer change the premium or cancel the coverage?</li><li>Does the coverage stay with me if I change jobs?</li></ul><p>If you aren't sure what your current coverage would actually pay, we're happy to read through the booklet or the policy with you.</p>
            """),
        new("Critical Illness Insurance in Plain Language",
            "Protection",
            "How critical illness insurance works, how it differs from disability and health coverage, and why the definitions in the contract matter.",
            """
            <p>Critical illness insurance is one of the simpler kinds of coverage to describe, and one of the easier ones to misunderstand.</p><h3>The basic idea</h3><p>A critical illness policy pays a single lump sum if you're diagnosed with one of the conditions listed in the contract and you meet the contract's terms. The list varies from one policy to another. Cancer, heart attack and stroke appear on most lists, and many contracts cover more.</p><p>The money is paid to you, not to a hospital or a lender. It isn't tied to receipts, and the contract generally doesn't tell you how to use it. There are conditions. Most contracts require that you survive for a stated period after the diagnosis, and the diagnosis has to meet the contract's own definition of the illness.</p><h3>How it differs from other coverage</h3><ul><li><strong>Disability insurance</strong> replaces part of your income, month by month, while you're unable to work. Critical illness insurance pays once, on diagnosis, whether or not you keep working.</li><li><strong>Provincial health coverage and workplace health benefits</strong> pay for medical care and some related costs. They don't replace income or cover everything a serious illness changes in a household.</li><li><strong>Life insurance</strong> pays when you die. Critical illness insurance is designed around surviving a serious illness.</li></ul><p>These types of coverage do different jobs, which is why some people hold more than one.</p><h3>What people use the money for</h3><p>People use the money for whatever the illness makes necessary or important:</p><ul><li>Covering the bills while they or a spouse take time away from work</li><li>Paying for treatment, medication or travel that public and workplace plans don't cover</li><li>Paying down debt to lower the household's monthly costs</li><li>Making changes to a home, or arranging help with care</li><li>Keeping a business running during an absence</li></ul><h3>Why the definitions matter</h3><p>A critical illness contract is only as clear as its definitions. A condition with a familiar name may be covered only at a certain severity, or only when specific medical criteria are met. Early-stage or less severe forms of an illness may be excluded, or may pay a smaller, partial benefit. Some contracts exclude conditions connected to your health history before the policy began. None of this is hidden, but it is all in the wording, and the wording differs between contracts.</p><h3>Questions to ask</h3><ul><li>Which conditions does this contract cover, and how does it define each one?</li><li>What survival period applies after a diagnosis?</li><li>Are there partial benefits for less severe conditions?</li><li>What is excluded, and how does my medical history affect coverage?</li><li>Can the premium change over time, and how long does the coverage last?</li><li>What happens to the premiums I've paid if I never make a claim?</li></ul><p>Tax treatment can vary with how a policy is owned and paid for, so that's a good question for us or a tax professional.</p><p>If you'd like to go through a contract, or the idea in general, we're always happy to sit down and talk it over.</p>
            """),
        new("Naming a Beneficiary: Small Form, Big Consequences",
            "Protection",
            "Why a beneficiary designation deserves more care than the form suggests, and when it is worth checking that yours is current.",
            """
            <p>Naming a beneficiary takes a few minutes and a signature, so it's easy to treat as a formality. It's actually one of the more important decisions attached to a life insurance policy or a registered plan, because it decides who receives the money and how smoothly it reaches them.</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>When a life insurance policy has a named beneficiary, the death benefit is generally paid directly to that person, outside of the estate. That usually means the money arrives sooner and isn't tied up while the estate is being settled. When no beneficiary is named, or the named person has already died, the proceeds typically go to the estate and are dealt with under the will or under provincial law.</p><p>Registered plans such as RRSPs and TFSAs can often carry a beneficiary designation too, although the rules for these are not the same in every province.</p><h3>Primary and contingent beneficiaries</h3><p>The <strong>primary beneficiary</strong> is the person, or people, first in line to receive the money. A <strong>contingent beneficiary</strong> receives it if the primary beneficiary has died before you. Naming one is a simple way to keep the proceeds from falling into the estate by accident.</p><p>You can name more than one person and set out the share each receives. A designation can also be revocable, which means you can change it on your own, or irrevocable, which means the beneficiary has to agree to any change.</p><h3>Keeping it current</h3><p>Beneficiary forms are often filled out once and forgotten. Life moves on and the form doesn't. These are the moments when a designation commonly falls out of date:</p><ul><li>Marriage, or the start of a common-law relationship</li><li>Separation or divorce</li><li>The birth or adoption of a child</li><li>The death of a beneficiary</li><li>A change of job, where workplace coverage comes with its own form</li></ul><p>A separation or a new will doesn't necessarily change who is named on a policy. The form on file with the insurer or plan provider is usually what counts, so it's worth knowing what yours says.</p><h3>Children and trustees</h3><p>Naming a minor child raises a practical problem: insurers generally can't pay a large sum directly to a child. Without other arrangements, the money may be held by a court or a public official until the child reaches the age of majority, which varies by province. Many people name a trustee to receive and manage the money on the child's behalf, or set up a trust through their will.</p><h3>Where the rules differ</h3><p>Beneficiary rules differ by province and by contract, and Quebec in particular has its own. Family law, a separation agreement or a court order can also affect who you're able to name. This article is general information, not legal advice. An estate lawyer can tell you how the rules apply to you, and we can help you find out what your current forms say.</p><p>If you'd like to check who is named on your policies and plans, we'd be glad to look through them with you.</p>
            """),
        new("What Happens in an Annual Review, and Why It Matters",
            "Planning",
            "What we look at in a yearly review, what to bring, and how a regular check catches small changes before they become problems.",
            """
            <p>An annual review is a scheduled conversation, usually once a year, to check that your coverage and your plans still fit your life. It isn't a sales meeting and it isn't an exam. Most of the time it confirms that things are on track. Its real value lies in the times it doesn't.</p><h3>What we look at</h3><ul><li><strong>What has changed.</strong> Family, work, health, home, income and debts. Even a change that seems small can affect how much coverage makes sense or who depends on you.</li><li><strong>Your insurance coverage.</strong> Whether the amounts and types still match the needs they were set up for, whether any term coverage is approaching a renewal or a conversion deadline, and whether the premiums are still comfortable.</li><li><strong>Your beneficiaries.</strong> Whether the people named on your policies and registered plans are still the people you intend.</li><li><strong>Your savings and investments.</strong> Whether your contributions, your time horizon and your comfort with risk still line up with your goals. We look at progress toward the goal, not at short-term market movements.</li><li><strong>Your workplace benefits.</strong> A new employer, or a change to a group plan, can open a gap or create an overlap.</li><li><strong>Your goals.</strong> Whether the goals themselves have changed. They often do.</li></ul><h3>What you bring</h3><p>Not much. Recent statements, any new policies or benefits booklets, and a rough sense of your income, savings and debts are plenty. Approximate figures are fine. The most useful thing you can bring is news: what has happened since we last spoke, and what you expect in the year ahead.</p><p>Questions are welcome too, including the ones that feel too small to ask. If a letter from an insurer confused you, or you read something that made you wonder about your own plan, the review is a good place to raise it.</p><h3>How small changes get caught early</h3><p>Financial plans rarely go wrong all at once. They drift. A beneficiary form still names a former spouse. A term policy nears the end of its conversion window without anyone noticing. A raise lifts a family's spending while the disability coverage stays based on the old income. A mortgage is paid off and the coverage built around it is no longer needed in the same form.</p><p>None of these is an emergency on the day it happens. Each is simple to deal with when it's noticed early, and harder, or impossible, to fix after a claim or a missed deadline. A regular review is how they get noticed.</p><h3>What happens afterward</h3><p>Often, nothing needs to change, and that's a perfectly good result. Knowing that your arrangements still fit is worth the time. When something does need attention, we explain the options and what each involves, and you decide what to do and when. Where tax or legal questions come up, we'll point you to a tax professional or a lawyer.</p><p>If it's been a while since your last review, or you've never had one, we'd be glad to set up a time that suits you.</p>
            """),
        new("Life Changes That Should Prompt a Call to Your Adviser",
            "Planning",
            "The life events that can affect your coverage and your plans, and what might need a look when each one happens.",
            """
            <p>A financial plan is built around your life as it is at one moment. When your life changes, parts of the plan may need to change with it. You don't have to work out what needs doing before you call. Letting us know that something has changed is enough.</p><p>These are the changes that most often matter.</p><h3>Marriage, a new partner, or a separation</h3><p>Joining your finances with someone else's raises questions about beneficiaries, shared debts and whether each person's coverage reflects who now depends on them. A separation raises the same questions in reverse, and agreements or court orders may set out insurance obligations. Beneficiary designations don't necessarily update themselves, and the rules differ by province.</p><h3>A child</h3><p>A birth or adoption usually means someone will rely on your income for many years. People often review their life and disability coverage at this point, along with their beneficiaries, their wills and how they might save for education.</p><h3>A home</h3><p>A mortgage is likely the largest debt a household takes on. It's a natural time to look at how the payments would be managed if an income were lost to death, illness or injury, and to be clear about what any insurance attached to the mortgage itself does and doesn't cover.</p><h3>A new job or a business</h3><p>Workplace benefits differ widely from one employer to another, and group coverage usually ends when you leave. Moving to self-employment can mean having no group coverage at all. Starting or buying a business brings its own questions about partners, key people, loans and what would happen to the business if an owner couldn't work.</p><h3>An inheritance or other windfall</h3><p>A sudden sum of money can change what's possible, and it often arrives at an emotional time. It's worth talking through before decisions are made, including any tax questions, which belong with a tax professional.</p><h3>A change in health</h3><p>A diagnosis, for you or someone in your family, can affect existing coverage, future insurability and the plan as a whole. If you have disability or critical illness coverage, we can help you understand what your contract says and how a claim works. Some existing policies also hold options that don't require new medical evidence.</p><h3>Approaching retirement</h3><p>The years before retirement shift the focus from building savings to drawing an income from them. Coverage needs often change as debts are paid off and children become independent. Questions about pensions, government benefits, registered plans and the estate all start to connect, and the order in which things are done can matter.</p><h3>Anything else that feels significant</h3><p>Caring for an aging parent, a move to another province, a large raise or a loss of income, a loan you've co-signed: if it feels significant, it's probably worth a mention. A short call is easy, and often the answer is that everything still fits.</p><p>Whenever something changes, we're glad to hear from you and to talk through what it might mean.</p>
            """),
        new("Understanding Risk Tolerance Before You Invest",
            "Planning",
            "Willingness and capacity to take risk are different things, and understanding both comes before any conversation about investment products.",
            """
            <p>Every investment conversation starts, or ought to start, with a question about you and not about the markets: how much uncertainty you can live with. The answer is usually called risk tolerance, and it has more parts than most people expect.</p><h3>Willingness and capacity are different things</h3><p><strong>Willingness</strong> to take risk is emotional. It's how you feel when the value of your savings moves up and down, and how well you sleep while it does.</p><p><strong>Capacity</strong> to take risk is financial. It's how much of a decline your plans could absorb without real damage. It depends on how steady your income is, what you've set aside for emergencies, what you owe, who depends on you, and how soon you'll need the money.</p><p>Willingness and capacity don't always match. Someone may be comfortable with big swings and yet have no room for a loss, because the money is needed soon. Someone else may have decades ahead and plenty of capacity, and still find any decline hard to bear. A sound plan respects both, and generally takes its cue from whichever is lower.</p><h3>Time horizon</h3><p>Time horizon is how long the money can stay invested before you need it. Money needed soon has little time to recover from a decline. Money that won't be touched for a long while has more. One person can have several time horizons at once, such as a home purchase that's close and a retirement that's far off, and each may call for its own approach.</p><h3>Why people misjudge themselves</h3><p>Risk tolerance is easy to overestimate when markets are calm. A questionnaire filled out in a good stretch asks how you'd feel about a decline in the abstract. Living through one is different: the numbers on the statement are your own, the news is alarming, and nobody can say when it will end.</p><p>The opposite mistake happens in falling markets. Fear makes people feel less tolerant of risk than they really are over the long term. In both cases, recent experience is doing the talking.</p><p>It helps to think about real moments, not hypothetical ones: how you reacted the last time your savings dropped, whether you lost sleep or wanted to sell. If you've never invested through a decline, your tolerance simply hasn't been tested yet.</p><h3>Why the conversation comes first</h3><p>Investments carry risk. Values rise and fall, and past performance doesn't tell you what will happen next. No product removes that uncertainty altogether. What a good conversation can do is make sure the risk you take on is risk you understand, can afford and can live with.</p><p>That's why we talk about your goals, your time horizons, your circumstances and your past experience before any product is discussed. Your answers aren't fixed. They change as your life does, which is why we come back to them over time.</p><p>If you'd like to talk through your own comfort with risk, with no product on the table, we'd welcome the conversation.</p>
            """),
        new("Getting Your Paperwork Ready for a Planning Meeting",
            "Planning",
            "A practical checklist of the documents and information that make a planning meeting more useful, with approximate figures welcome.",
            """
            <p>A planning meeting goes further when we can see the whole picture. That doesn't mean arriving with a perfect file. It means gathering what's easy to find, noting what isn't, and letting us help with the rest.</p><p>Here's the kind of information that helps. Bring what you have, and don't worry about what you can't find.</p><h3>A checklist</h3><ul><li><strong>Existing insurance policies.</strong> Life, disability, critical illness and any other personal coverage. The policy itself or a recent statement is ideal, and the insurer's name and policy number are enough to start with.</li><li><strong>Workplace benefits booklet.</strong> The summary of your group coverage, including life, disability and health benefits, and a recent statement for any pension or group savings plan. If you have a spouse or partner, theirs as well.</li><li><strong>Investment and savings statements.</strong> Recent statements for RRSPs, TFSAs, education savings, non-registered accounts and bank savings.</li><li><strong>Debts.</strong> Mortgage, lines of credit, car loans, student loans and credit cards, with the approximate balance and payment for each.</li><li><strong>Income and spending.</strong> A recent pay stub or a rough idea of household income, plus a sense of what it costs to run your household each month.</li><li><strong>Tax documents.</strong> Your most recent tax return and notice of assessment can answer many questions at once.</li><li><strong>Wills and powers of attorney.</strong> We don't need to read them. It helps to know whether they exist and roughly when they were last updated.</li><li><strong>Beneficiary information.</strong> Who is named on your policies and registered plans, if you know.</li><li><strong>Business documents.</strong> If you own a business, a recent financial statement and any shareholder or partnership agreement.</li><li><strong>Your goals.</strong> What you want the next few years, and the long run, to look like. A few lines on paper are enough.</li><li><strong>Your questions.</strong> Anything you've been wondering about, however small.</li></ul><h3>Approximate figures are fine</h3><p>You don't need exact numbers to have a useful conversation. A rough balance, a ballpark income and an honest guess at monthly spending will take us most of the way. If something matters down to the last digit, we can confirm it later. There's no need to put off a meeting because the paperwork isn't in order. Helping to organize it is part of what we do.</p><h3>A note on privacy</h3><p>The documents on this list hold sensitive personal information, and we handle what you share with care. If you'd like to send anything ahead of time, ask us first about a secure way to do it, since ordinary email isn't always the right choice for financial documents.</p><h3>What happens with it</h3><p>We use what you bring to understand where things stand today: what you own, what you owe, what's protected and what isn't. From there we can talk about what you'd like to change. Nothing you bring commits you to anything.</p><p>If you have questions about what to gather, or you'd just like to book a time, we're happy to hear from you.</p>
            """),
    };

    public static readonly GenericStarterArticles.Entry[] Mortgage =
    {
        new("How Much Home Can You Comfortably Afford?",
            "Home Buying Basics",
            "What a lender may approve and what feels comfortable month to month are different questions, and a good home search starts with the second.",
            """
            <p>When people start looking at homes, the first number they usually hear is the amount a lender may be willing to approve. It's a useful number, but it answers the lender's question, not yours. Yours is simpler and more personal: what payment can you live with, month after month, without feeling squeezed.</p><h3>Approved and comfortable are not the same thing</h3><p>Lenders work from set formulas. They compare your income with your expected housing costs and your other debts, and they test whether you could still manage if rates were higher than the rate you're offered.</p><p>What the formulas can't see is the rest of your life: child care, a long commute, the trips you take to see family, the savings you don't want to stop, a plan to change careers or grow your family. A figure that works on paper can still feel tight in practice. A pre-approval, if you have one, is also only an early estimate. It isn't a commitment to lend, and it isn't a target to spend up to.</p><h3>What goes into the picture</h3><ul><li><strong>Your income.</strong> How much it is and how steady. Salaried, commissioned and self-employed income are each assessed a little differently.</li><li><strong>Your existing debts.</strong> Car loans, student loans, credit cards and lines of credit all take up part of your monthly budget before a mortgage payment does.</li><li><strong>Your down payment.</strong> Its size affects how much you borrow and whether mortgage default insurance is required, which adds a cost of its own.</li><li><strong>Closing costs.</strong> Legal fees, land transfer tax where it applies, and other one-time costs come out of your savings on top of the down payment.</li><li><strong>The running costs of a home.</strong> Property tax, heating, utilities, home insurance, condo fees if there are any, and the repairs every home eventually needs.</li><li><strong>Room for change.</strong> Rates may be different when your term comes up for renewal. With a variable rate, depending on the product, either your payment changes when rates move or the payment stays the same and the share going to principal changes. Life changes too: a leave from work, a new job, a car that needs replacing.</li></ul><h3>Why we start with your budget, not the listing</h3><p>It's easy to fall for a home first and try to make the numbers fit afterwards. We find the conversation goes better the other way around. We start with what comes in and what goes out each month, settle on a housing cost that leaves you some room, and work back from there to a price range.</p><p>The right amount is different for every household, and only you can decide what comfortable means. Our part is to lay out the costs clearly, explain how lenders will look at your situation, and answer your questions so the decision is an informed one.</p><p>If you'd like to talk through your own budget before you start looking, we're glad to sit down with you whenever you're ready.</p>
            """),
        new("Term and Amortization: Two Words People Mix Up",
            "Home Buying Basics",
            "Amortization is how long the whole mortgage takes to repay, while the term is how long your current rate and conditions last before renewal.",
            """
            <p>Mortgage paperwork is full of unfamiliar language, and these words cause more confusion than most. Both describe a length of time and they often appear side by side, but they mean quite different things.</p><h3>Amortization: the life of the loan</h3><p>Your amortization is the total length of time it would take to pay the mortgage off completely, assuming you make every regular payment and nothing else changes. The lender uses it, along with your rate and the amount you borrow, to work out your regular payment.</p><h3>Term: the contract you're in right now</h3><p>Your term is the length of your current mortgage contract. It sets how long your interest rate, or the way your rate is calculated, and the rest of your conditions stay in place. Terms run from a few months to several years, and they're usually much shorter than the amortization.</p><p>When a term ends, whatever you still owe comes up for renewal. You can usually renew with the same lender, move the mortgage elsewhere, change it or pay it off. So most mortgages are repaid over a series of terms, each with its own rate and conditions, inside a single amortization.</p><h3>What changes when the amortization is shorter or longer</h3><p>A shorter amortization means higher regular payments, because you're repaying the same amount in less time. In exchange, you pay less interest over the life of the mortgage and build equity sooner. A longer amortization lowers each payment and leaves more room in the monthly budget, but interest is charged over a longer period, so the total cost of borrowing is higher.</p><p>How long an amortization you can have depends on the rules in force when you apply, your down payment and the lender, so ask what applies to you.</p><h3>What changes when the term is shorter or longer</h3><p>A shorter term brings you back to renewal sooner. That gives you more frequent chances to make changes, and it also means your rate is reset more often, for better or worse. A longer term keeps your rate, or the way it's calculated, and your conditions in place for longer. The trade-off is less flexibility: if you need to leave a closed mortgage before the term ends, a prepayment charge usually applies, and how it's calculated depends on your contract.</p><p>A related wrinkle: depending on the product, a variable-rate mortgage either changes your payment when rates move, or keeps the payment the same and changes the share going to principal. Where the payment stays the same, the time left to repay can stretch or shrink as rates move.</p><h3>How they work together</h3><p>Each renewal is a natural time to look at both. You can keep the amortization on track, shorten it with higher payments or prepayments, or ask whether lengthening it is possible, which often means qualifying again. Neither choice is right for everyone.</p><p>If you'd like us to walk through how term and amortization would play out in your situation, we're happy to have that conversation.</p>
            """),
        new("The Costs of Buying a Home Beyond the Down Payment",
            "Home Buying Basics",
            "The down payment is the largest upfront cost of buying, but several one-time costs arrive around closing and are easier to handle when planned early.",
            """
            <p>Most buyers spend a long time saving a down payment, and it's easy to treat that figure as the whole cost of buying. Another set of costs arrives around closing day. None of them is a surprise if you know to expect it, and planning early keeps your savings from being stretched at the last minute.</p><h3>The usual one-time costs</h3><ul><li><strong>Legal fees.</strong> A lawyer, or a notary in some provinces, searches the title, registers the transfer and the mortgage, and handles the money on closing day. The bill includes their fee plus charges such as registration and search costs.</li><li><strong>Land transfer tax.</strong> Most provinces charge a tax or a registration fee when a property changes hands, and some municipalities add their own. Some offer relief for eligible first-time buyers. Where it applies, it's paid at closing from your own funds.</li><li><strong>Home inspection.</strong> Paid for by the buyer, usually before the purchase becomes firm. It's your choice in most cases, and the report can tell you a lot about the repairs a home may need.</li><li><strong>Appraisal.</strong> A lender may require an independent opinion of the property's value. Sometimes the lender covers the cost and sometimes the borrower does.</li><li><strong>Title insurance.</strong> A one-time premium for a policy that protects against certain problems with the property's title. Many lenders require it.</li><li><strong>Adjustments.</strong> If the seller has prepaid property tax, condo fees or utilities past the closing date, you reimburse your share. Your lawyer or notary sets these out in a statement of adjustments.</li><li><strong>Mortgage default insurance.</strong> Where it's required, the premium is normally added to the mortgage rather than paid up front. In some provinces a sales tax applies to that premium, and the tax is typically due at closing.</li><li><strong>Home insurance.</strong> Lenders require it to be in place by closing, so the first premium often lands at the same time.</li><li><strong>Moving and setting up.</strong> Movers or a rented truck, utility connections, new locks, and the things a new place seems to need right away.</li></ul><h3>Why the totals differ so much</h3><p>We've left figures out on purpose. The amounts depend on the province, sometimes the municipality, the purchase price, the type of property and the professionals you hire. A newly built home can bring costs a resale home doesn't, such as sales tax on the purchase and adjustments in the builder's agreement. Tax rules and rebate programs also change, so a friend's experience may not match yours.</p><h3>Planning for them</h3><p>Lenders generally want to see that you have funds for closing costs on top of your down payment, so these costs matter to your application as well as to your bank account. It helps to ask early for written estimates from the lawyer or notary and anyone else you'll be paying, and to keep that money separate from the down payment.</p><p>If you'd like help putting together a realistic estimate for the kind of purchase you have in mind, we'd be glad to go through it with you.</p>
            """),
        new("Your Credit History and Your Mortgage: What Lenders Look At",
            "Home Buying Basics",
            "Learn what a credit report shows, which habits shape it, and why a thin or bruised history is not a closed door.",
            """
            <p>When you apply for a mortgage, the lender wants to know how you've handled borrowed money so far. Your credit report is where they look. It helps to know what's in it before someone else reads it.</p><h3>What a credit report shows</h3><p>Canada's credit bureaus collect information from the companies you deal with. A report typically lists your credit cards, loans and lines of credit, when each was opened, its limit and balance, and whether payments arrived on time. It also records inquiries from lenders when you've applied for credit, accounts sent to collections, and events such as a bankruptcy or consumer proposal.</p><p>From this, the bureaus calculate a credit score. Lenders use it, but they read the report behind it as well, alongside your income, your down payment and the property itself. The score is part of the picture, not the whole of it.</p><h3>The habits that shape it</h3><ul><li>Paying on time, every time, including small bills. Payment history carries a lot of weight.</li><li>Keeping balances well below your limits rather than running cards close to the top.</li><li>Keeping older accounts in good standing. A long, steady record helps.</li><li>Applying for new credit only when you need it, since a cluster of applications in a short time can count against you.</li><li>Dealing with overdue accounts rather than letting them sit.</li></ul><h3>Check your own report</h3><p>You're entitled to ask each bureau for a copy of your report, and checking your own file doesn't affect your score. Mistakes happen: an account that isn't yours, a debt shown as unpaid after you settled it, an old address tied to someone with a similar name. Each bureau has a process for disputing errors, and it can take time, so looking early is useful.</p><h3>Why new borrowing matters around an application</h3><p>A lender assesses you as you are on the day you apply, and many check again before the mortgage is advanced. Financing a vehicle, opening a store card for furniture or co-signing for a relative in between changes your debts and adds inquiries to your file. It doesn't always cause a problem, but it can, which is why it helps to ask before taking on anything new between application and closing.</p><h3>A thin or bruised history</h3><p>Some people have very little credit history because they're new to Canada, young, or have always paid cash. Others have a rough patch behind them. Neither ends the conversation. Lenders differ in what they'll consider, history can be built or rebuilt with time and steady habits, and other strengths in an application count too. The options may be different, and we can explain what they are and what they cost.</p><p>If you're unsure how your credit history would look to a lender, we're happy to talk it through with you, with no judgment and no obligation.</p>
            """),
        new("Your Renewal Is Coming: What to Do Before You Sign",
            "Managing Your Mortgage",
            "A renewal offer is an offer, not an obligation, and the months before your term ends are a good time to review your mortgage.",
            """
            <p>Near the end of your mortgage term, your lender will send a renewal offer. It often comes with a form that's quick to sign and return, and many people do exactly that. There's nothing wrong with renewing where you are, but the offer is a starting place, and you have more choices at renewal than at almost any other time.</p><h3>An offer, not an obligation</h3><p>When a term ends, the contract that set your rate and conditions ends with it. You can accept the lender's offer, ask whether it can be improved, change the type or length of the term, or move the mortgage to another lender. Moving usually means a new application, and the new lender will look at your income, credit and property under the rules in place at the time. There may be costs to switch, and sometimes they're covered, so ask.</p><p>If you do nothing, what happens depends on your lender and your contract. Some mortgages renew automatically into a term you didn't pick, which is a good reason to read the offer closely and note its deadline.</p><h3>Start the conversation early</h3><p>Time is the most useful thing to have at renewal. Starting a few months ahead leaves room to gather documents, ask questions and compare without a deadline pressing on you. Some lenders allow an early renewal within a set window before the term ends; the details vary by lender.</p><h3>What has changed since last time</h3><p>The mortgage you chose at the start of this term suited your life then. It's worth a few minutes to think about what's different now:</p><ul><li>your income, your household or your other debts</li><li>plans to move, renovate or buy another property during the next term</li><li>savings you might put against the balance, since renewal is typically a time when you can pay down principal or change your payment without a prepayment charge</li><li>how much rate movement you're comfortable living with</li></ul><h3>What to compare besides the rate</h3><ul><li><strong>Prepayment privileges.</strong> How much extra you can pay each year, and how, without a charge.</li><li><strong>Portability.</strong> Whether you can take the mortgage with you to a new home, and on what conditions.</li><li><strong>The charge for leaving early.</strong> How the prepayment charge is calculated if you break the term. Methods differ between lenders and between fixed and variable rates, and the difference can be large.</li><li><strong>The type and length of term.</strong> Fixed or variable, shorter or longer, and how each fits your plans.</li></ul><p>A lower rate with conditions that don't fit your plans isn't always the lower-cost choice in the end. Which combination suits you depends on what the next few years are likely to hold, and that's your call to make.</p><p>If your renewal is coming up and you'd like to go over the offer and your options together, we'd be glad to hear from you.</p>
            """),
        new("Refinancing: When It Helps and When It Does Not",
            "Managing Your Mortgage",
            "Refinancing can consolidate debt, fund renovations or reshape a mortgage, but the costs can outweigh the benefit, so the details decide it.",
            """
            <p>Refinancing means replacing your current mortgage with a new one, usually to borrow more against your home, change the amortization or change the terms. It can be a sensible tool. It can also cost more than it saves. The difference is in the details of your contract and your reasons.</p><h3>Why people refinance</h3><ul><li><strong>Consolidating debt.</strong> Rolling credit card or loan balances into the mortgage can lower the interest rate on that debt and replace several payments with a single payment.</li><li><strong>Renovations.</strong> Using the equity in your home to pay for major work, rather than a loan or line of credit that may carry a higher rate.</li><li><strong>A better fit.</strong> Changing the amortization, the payment or the type of mortgage because your income, household or plans have changed.</li><li><strong>A large expense.</strong> Education, helping a family member or another significant cost.</li></ul><h3>What it can cost</h3><p>If you refinance before your term ends, a prepayment charge usually applies. How it's calculated depends on your contract and on whether your rate is fixed or variable, and it can be substantial. Your lender can tell you the current amount.</p><p>There are also legal fees to register the new mortgage, often an appraisal fee, and sometimes a fee to discharge the old mortgage. You'll need to qualify again under the rules in place now, and there are limits on how much of your home's value you can borrow against.</p><h3>The less obvious trade-offs</h3><p>Moving short-term debt into a mortgage usually means repaying it over a much longer time. The payment drops, but the total interest on that debt may end up higher, not lower. The debt also becomes secured by your home. And if the spending that built the balances doesn't change, they can return alongside a larger mortgage.</p><p>Borrowing against your home for a renovation adds to what you owe, and the work may or may not add the same amount to the home's value.</p><h3>The questions that decide it</h3><ul><li>What does it cost, in total, to make the change now?</li><li>How does that compare with waiting until renewal, when a prepayment charge typically doesn't apply?</li><li>What is the total cost over time, not only the monthly payment?</li><li>Are there other ways to reach the same goal, such as a secured line of credit, or a lender's option to blend your existing rate with a new one?</li><li>How long do you expect to stay in the home?</li></ul><p>Sometimes the arithmetic clearly favours refinancing, sometimes it clearly doesn't, and often it's close enough that your own comfort and plans settle it. We can help you lay the figures side by side; the decision stays with you.</p><p>If you're wondering whether refinancing makes sense in your situation, we're happy to look at it with you, with no obligation.</p>
            """),
        new("How Extra Payments Shorten a Mortgage",
            "Managing Your Mortgage",
            "Extra payments go straight to principal, which lowers the interest charged from then on and brings the end of the mortgage closer.",
            """
            <p>Every regular mortgage payment is split. Part covers the interest that has built up since your last payment, and the rest reduces the amount you owe, which is the principal. Early in a mortgage, interest takes a large share. As the balance falls, more of each payment goes to principal.</p><h3>Why a prepayment works harder than it looks</h3><p>A prepayment is money you pay on top of your regular payments. Because the interest for the period has already been covered, a prepayment goes straight to principal. From that day on, interest is calculated on a smaller balance, so a little more of every later payment goes to principal too. The effect builds on itself, and it's stronger the earlier in the mortgage it happens.</p><p>The result is a mortgage that's paid off sooner and costs less in interest overall. How much sooner depends on the amounts, the timing and your rate.</p><h3>The usual kinds of prepayment privilege</h3><p>Most closed mortgages allow some prepayment without a charge. The common forms are:</p><ul><li><strong>Lump sums.</strong> An extra amount paid against the principal, up to a yearly limit. Some contracts allow it on any payment date, others only at certain times.</li><li><strong>Increased payments.</strong> Raising your regular payment by up to a set limit. The whole increase goes to principal.</li><li><strong>More frequent payments.</strong> Moving from monthly to weekly or every other week. An accelerated schedule shortens the mortgage mainly because you end up paying somewhat more over a year than you would monthly.</li></ul><p>An open mortgage lets you prepay any amount at any time, usually in exchange for a higher rate.</p><h3>The limits are in your contract</h3><p>Privileges differ from lender to lender and from mortgage to mortgage. The limits, the dates on which you can use them, and whether unused room carries forward are all set out in your contract. In many contracts it doesn't carry forward. Going over the limit on a closed mortgage usually triggers a prepayment charge, so it's worth confirming the details with your lender before sending a large amount.</p><h3>Weighing it against other goals</h3><p>Extra payments compete with other uses for the same money. Money put into the mortgage isn't easy to get back if you need it later, so many people look first at whether they have savings for emergencies. Debts at higher interest rates, retirement savings and a child's education are other common priorities. There's no single right order. It depends on your rates, your tax situation, your plans and how you feel about carrying debt.</p><p>If you'd like to see what your own prepayment privileges allow and how extra payments might change your timeline, we'd be happy to walk through it with you.</p>
            """),
        new("What to Expect When You Work With a Mortgage Professional",
            "Managing Your Mortgage",
            "From the first conversation to closing day, here is how the process usually unfolds and which documents you can expect to be asked for.",
            """
            <p>Arranging a mortgage can feel like a process that happens to you. It doesn't need to. Knowing the steps ahead of time makes it easier to ask questions at the right moments and to feel in charge of your own decision.</p><h3>The first conversation</h3><p>The first conversation is mostly us listening. We'll ask what you're hoping to do, whether that's buying, renewing or refinancing, and about your income, savings, debts and timing. You don't need paperwork in hand or a firm plan. The aim is to understand where you stand and to explain, in plain language, how lenders are likely to look at your situation.</p><p>If a pre-approval makes sense, we'll explain what it is and what it isn't. A pre-approval is an early assessment based on the information available. It is not a commitment to lend; the lender's decision comes after it has reviewed your full application and the property.</p><h3>The documents typically asked for</h3><ul><li><strong>Proof of income.</strong> Recent pay stubs and a letter of employment, or tax documents and financial statements if you're self-employed.</li><li><strong>Down payment history.</strong> Account statements covering the past few months, and a signed gift letter if family is helping.</li><li><strong>Identification.</strong> Government-issued photo identification for each applicant.</li><li><strong>Details of debts.</strong> Balances and payments for loans, credit cards, lines of credit and any support payments.</li><li><strong>Property details.</strong> The purchase agreement and listing, or your current mortgage statement and property tax bill if you already own.</li></ul><p>You'll also be asked for consent before anyone checks your credit report. Lenders ask for these things because they're required to verify what's in an application, and requirements vary from lender to lender.</p><h3>How options are explained</h3><p>Once we understand your situation, we'll go through the options open to you: the type of rate, the term, the amortization, prepayment privileges, and what it would cost to leave early. We'll explain the trade-offs in words that make sense, including the parts that are less convenient to hear. The choice is yours, and you can take the time you need to make it.</p><h3>Between approval and closing</h3><p>An approval usually comes with conditions, such as an appraisal, confirmation of your documents or proof of home insurance. Once those are met, the lender sends instructions to your lawyer or notary, you sign the mortgage documents, and the funds are advanced on closing day.</p><p>Lenders can re-check employment and credit before funding, so it helps to tell us before you change jobs or take on new credit during this stretch.</p><h3>Questions are welcome throughout</h3><p>Nobody arranges mortgages often enough to remember how it all works. Ask about anything, as many times as you need to, at any stage.</p><p>If you'd like to know what the process would look like for you, we're always glad to start with a conversation.</p>
            """),
    };
}
